  "limitations": [
    "Odometry is oracle-assisted (true increments plus noise); the object map and every claim position are in that odometry frame. The scorer maps them to the world with the true pose at the end of the task (drift compensation for scoring only).",
    "Physics is frozen during inference (synchronous simulation): the robot is held while Qwen decides or detects. A real-time deployment is not measured.",
    "l_shape is a development kitchen (the closeout, the toolbox and the Isaac bring-up were developed on it); g_shape, l_island, peninsula and u_shape are held-out for every Qwen run. They were used before only by the classical, non-model explorer (results/isaac_g1_kitchen_*). The semantic modules and their thresholds were not tuned on any of them.",
    "The detector is Qwen3-VL grounding boxes, in a coordinate convention (relative 0..1000 versus absolute pixels) measured on l_shape before freezing (results/isaac_semantic_dev/coordinate_check.json).",
    "Ground truth for the independent scorer is the NVIDIA scene's own semantic labels reduced to axis-aligned boxes (sink as the union of its rim and basin prims, countertop). THE REFRIGERATOR AND THE OVEN ARE ABSENT FROM EVERY KITCHEN: the scene declares prims labelled refrigerator and oven, but their SimReady asset references (../../SimReady/Residential/Kitchen/Appliances/...) return HTTP 404 on NVIDIA's asset server for Isaac 6.2, so those prims carry no geometry, no collider and no bound (results/isaac_semantic_dev/scene_probe_5_kitchens.json). 'Find/Walk to the refrigerator' are therefore absent-target requests, exactly like 'Find the bed': the only correct outcome is no claim, and a refrigerator success is impossible by construction. This is an environment limitation found after the 7 Nov closeout (whose T3 refrigerator task was likewise unanswerable); only the sink can be found. No kettle exists either; 'Pick up the kettle' is unsupported by capability.",
    "Planner memory hints are empty: a live Isaac session has no home memory. The semantic world attaches only to FindObject goals; Inspect and refusal paths keep the closeout toolbox.",
    "The robot-time budget is counted from session start (including the 10 s stand-up), so the usable task time is about 290 s. 60 tool calls at most.",
    "One run per case (no repetition), so success counts are small-sample point values without confidence intervals; two starts per kitchen."
  ],
